# Vulnerability details

## Impact
Due to incorrect validation of `globalData.lockData.locker` in the `GlobalData.sol#initializeLock()` function, once `globalData.lockData.locker` is set, this function cannot be accessed again.
However, since the `initializeLock()` function is called from several core functions of `Predy`, the protocol cannot perform its function. In other words, the protocol is in a situation where it can go bankrupt.

## Proof of Concept
The `GlobalData.sol#initializeLock()` is the function that Initializes lock for token settlement.
```solidity
    function initializeLock(GlobalDataLibrary.GlobalData storage globalData, uint256 pairId) internal {
36:     if (globalData.lockData.locker != address(0)) {
37:         revert IPredyPool.LockedBy(globalData.lockData.locker);
38:     }

        globalData.lockData.quoteReserve = ERC20(globalData.pairs[pairId].quotePool.token).balanceOf(address(this));
        globalData.lockData.baseReserve = ERC20(globalData.pairs[pairId].basePool.token).balanceOf(address(this));
42:     globalData.lockData.locker = msg.sender;
        globalData.lockData.pairId = pairId;
    }
```
As shown in the code snippet above, once `globalData.lockData.locker` is set in #L42, it is always DOSed by #L36~#L38.

On the other hand, the `GlobalData.sol#initializeLock()` function is called from the following core functions of the protocol.

1.[PredyPool.sol#trade(): This is the function that trades perps and squarts.](https://github.com/code-423n4/2024-05-predy/blob/main/src/PredyPool.sol#L265-L279)

PredyPool.sol#trade():
```solidity
